# Traffic Server's HTTP state machine buffers a request body across a
# redirect (proxy/http/HttpSM.cc at the parent of d3ef5233): the buffered
# copy lives in _postbuf, whose postdata_copy_buffer_start is an
# IOBufferReader* that PostDataBuffers::clear() sets to nullptr after
# freeing the buffer (HttpSM.cc:8960-8961). A POST carrying
# "Expect: 100-continue" reaches tunnel_handler_100_continue, which
# destroys the response header, deallocates the tunnel buffers, calls
# this->postbuf_clear() -- nulling postdata_copy_buffer_start -- and
# then, when the server is still open, calls
# do_setup_client_request_body_tunnel (HttpSM.cc:3049-3081):
#
#   t_state.hdr_info.client_response.destroy();
#   tunnel.deallocate_buffers();
#   this->postbuf_clear();
#   tunnel.reset();
#   ...
#   } else {
#     do_setup_client_request_body_tunnel(HttpVC_t::SERVER_VC);
#   }
#
# is_buffering_request_body is still set, so the FIRST arm of the redirect
# condition fires and copies from the nulled reader without the null test
# the sibling arm carries (HttpSM.cc:6414-6421):
#
#   if (is_buffering_request_body ||
#       (t_state.redirect_info.redirect_in_process && enable_redirection &&
#        this->_postbuf.postdata_copy_buffer_start != nullptr)) {
#     MIOBuffer *postdata_producer_buffer = new_empty_MIOBuffer(...);
#     ...
#     postdata_producer_buffer->write(this->_postbuf.postdata_copy_buffer_start);
#
# MIOBuffer::write(IOBufferReader *r) evaluates r->block before anything
# else (iocore/eventsystem/IOBuffer.cc:191-193), so the proxy dereferences
# null in-process and crashes: a remote DoS (CWE-476) reachable with
# request_buffer_enabled and number_of_redirections >= 1, reproduced by
# the fixed revision's own autest (tests/gold_tests/post/simple-post-valid-buffer-
# check.test.py, "This make ats crash"). The fixed revision d3ef5233 removes
# the postbuf_clear() call from the 100-continue handler and guards both
# arms of the condition with _postbuf.is_valid() (the header's spelling of
# postdata_copy_buffer_start != nullptr).
#
# What fires on the real code: nothing, on either revision, at the current
# definitions state. Bounded scans of src/proxy/http (timeout 180,
# --max-ram 6GB, --cache off), three runs per side, report 317 findings on
# the parent and 317 on the fixed revision, byte-stable inside each
# revision. Every one of the 17 rows the scans place in HttpSM.cc has an
# exact counterpart across the revision boundary -- 13 shifted up one line
# by the removed call, 4 at the same line -- so no finding appears or
# disappears, and nothing touches HttpSM.cc:3066, 6415 or 6421. Those 17
# rows are NullableDeref at 1208/1244/1883/1933/3850/3868/3872/4836/4876/
# 5748, RawMemoryCopySize at 7852/8513/8522/8548/8578, and a FileCheck/
# FileUse pair at 7719/7760, all on other functions' safe code: findings
# in the file are not coverage. The review-flag view (-flags with) holds
# the same boundary: the memory category carries no flag on HttpSM.cc
# between 619 and 6853, and the nearest resource flag below the removed
# clear call is code.OwnerFieldClear at 2885, below the use 6262.
#
# The invalidation itself IS labelled, identically at both revisions:
# the this->postdata_copy_buffer = nullptr / postdata_copy_buffer_start =
# nullptr writes inside PostDataBuffers::clear() lower to
# analysis.field.clear_null and flag code.OwnerFieldClear at
# HttpSM.cc:8960-8961 -- but the only rule consuming that concept is the
# leak-direction VYQL-RES-002 (clear dominates a free), and nothing orders
# it against a later use. The use side carries no label at all:
# MIOBuffer::write(IOBufferReader*) is a state-machine-internal API with
# no sink binding, and C++ emits no receiver-type fact that could confine
# a write-on-reader binding to the class.
#
# The blocker is measured, not assumed, with a minimal pair built on probe
# bindings labelling postbuf_clear() as code.PointerFree and the
# postdata_producer_buffer->write(...) call as code.PointerUse (VYQL-MEM-
# 007 is PointerFree reaches PointerUse): with free and use in ONE
# function the rule fires 1 finding; with the free in the 100-continue
# handler and the use in the callee it directly calls -- the real shape --
# it fires 0. Two stacked engine facts produce that zero, both re-measured
# at the current state. First, the callee never registers under its name:
# HttpSM::do_setup_client_request_body_tunnel is an out-of-line member
# definition, declName (internal/extract/frontend/treesitter/c.go) has no
# qualified_identifier case and returns "" for one, so the body registers
# as an anonymous frame and `vyql resolve` lists the call site as
# unresolved -- the call edge does not exist. Second, even with an edge,
# Reaches is region-comparability (internal/solvers/cfg.go) and two
# functions' regions are siblings, so no order rule can relate a release
# in the caller to a use in the callee.
#
# The callee-side binding route is spelling-keyed and is rejected on
# measurement, both halves re-run at the current state. A function-context
# binding on uses of postdata_copy_buffer_start without is_valid scores 1
# on the vulnerable form and 0 on the fixed one -- and 2 on the identical
# fix with is_valid renamed to valid (the tunnel-setup function plus the
# renamed guard's own body), so it discriminates by the remediation's
# method name. Keying the negation on the inline null test instead inverts
# the result: the vulnerable form carries
# postdata_copy_buffer_start != nullptr on the sibling arm of the ||, so a
# not-contains-nullptr binding scores 0 on the vulnerable form and 1 on
# the fixed one, which replaced that inline test with is_valid(). What
# actually distinguishes the revisions is that the null exclusion sits on
# the sibling arm of the || rather than the arm that governs the use, and
# branch-arm guard correlation is a third absent relation. A caller-side
# route does discriminate -- a function-context binding requiring both the
# postbuf_clear and the do_setup_client_request_body_tunnel identifiers
# scores 1 (the 100-continue handler) against 0 on the fixed tree -- but it
# names two private methods of this one state machine, so it is a
# repro-shaped patch rather than a detection, and it says nothing about any
# other buffer a caller invalidates before a helper uses it.
#
# The shape's reachable set is confined to HttpSM.cc (exhaustive grep over
# the scanned subtree): four postbuf_clear() call sites (3066, 3116, 7627,
# 8431), six postdata_copy_buffer_start mentions (6415, 6421, 8929, 8943,
# 8945, 8961), two _postbuf.init sites (870, 6443).

test "rank1868: a request-body buffer cleared in the 100-continue handler and written from in the tunnel setup it calls"
  lang cpp
  file src/proxy/http/HttpSM.cc
  reject VYQL-MEM-007
  reject VYQL-MEM-006
  code
  ```
  class IOBufferReader;
  class MIOBuffer {
  public:
    void write(IOBufferReader *r);
  };

  class PostDataBuffers {
  public:
    MIOBuffer      *postdata_copy_buffer       = nullptr;
    IOBufferReader *postdata_copy_buffer_start = nullptr;
    void clear();
    bool is_valid() { return postdata_copy_buffer_start != nullptr; }
  };

  class HttpSM {
    PostDataBuffers _postbuf;
    bool is_buffering_request_body = false;
    bool enable_redirection = false;
    bool redirect_in_process = false;
    void postbuf_clear() { _postbuf.clear(); }
    void do_setup_client_request_body_tunnel();
    void tunnel_handler_100_continue(bool eos);
  };

  void HttpSM::do_setup_client_request_body_tunnel()
  {
    if (is_buffering_request_body ||
        (redirect_in_process && enable_redirection &&
         this->_postbuf.postdata_copy_buffer_start != nullptr)) {
      MIOBuffer *postdata_producer_buffer = new MIOBuffer();
      postdata_producer_buffer->write(this->_postbuf.postdata_copy_buffer_start);
    }
  }

  void HttpSM::tunnel_handler_100_continue(bool eos)
  {
    this->postbuf_clear();
    if (!eos) {
      do_setup_client_request_body_tunnel();
    }
  }
  ```

test "rank1868: the fixed form keeps the buffer valid and checks it on both arms"
  lang cpp
  file src/proxy/http/HttpSM.cc
  reject VYQL-MEM-007
  reject VYQL-MEM-006
  code
  ```
  class IOBufferReader;
  class MIOBuffer {
  public:
    void write(IOBufferReader *r);
  };

  class PostDataBuffers {
  public:
    MIOBuffer      *postdata_copy_buffer       = nullptr;
    IOBufferReader *postdata_copy_buffer_start = nullptr;
    void clear();
    bool is_valid() { return postdata_copy_buffer_start != nullptr; }
  };

  class HttpSM {
    PostDataBuffers _postbuf;
    bool is_buffering_request_body = false;
    bool enable_redirection = false;
    bool redirect_in_process = false;
    void postbuf_clear() { _postbuf.clear(); }
    void do_setup_client_request_body_tunnel();
    void tunnel_handler_100_continue(bool eos);
  };

  void HttpSM::do_setup_client_request_body_tunnel()
  {
    if ((is_buffering_request_body && this->_postbuf.is_valid()) ||
        (redirect_in_process && enable_redirection && this->_postbuf.is_valid())) {
      MIOBuffer *postdata_producer_buffer = new MIOBuffer();
      postdata_producer_buffer->write(this->_postbuf.postdata_copy_buffer_start);
    }
  }

  void HttpSM::tunnel_handler_100_continue(bool eos)
  {
    if (!eos) {
      do_setup_client_request_body_tunnel();
    }
  }
  ```

test "rank1868: the same clear and write in one function is the pair the order rule covers"
  lang cpp
  file src/proxy/http/HttpSM.cc
  reject VYQL-MEM-007
  code
  ```
  class IOBufferReader;
  class MIOBuffer {
  public:
    void write(IOBufferReader *r);
  };
  class PostDataBuffers {
  public:
    void clear();
    IOBufferReader *postdata_copy_buffer_start = nullptr;
  };
  class HttpSM {
    PostDataBuffers _postbuf;
    void postbuf_clear() { _postbuf.clear(); }
    void handler();
  };
  void HttpSM::handler()
  {
    this->postbuf_clear();
    MIOBuffer *b = new MIOBuffer();
    b->write(this->_postbuf.postdata_copy_buffer_start);
  }
  ```
